      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by simplifying the given equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2}\\). First, we perform polynomial long division on the numerator \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) by the denominator \\(x + 2\\).\n\n1. Divide the leading term of the numerator \\(x^3\\) by the leading term of the denominator \\(x\\) to get \\(x^2\\).\n2. Multiply \\(x^2\\) by \\(x + 2\\) to get \\(x^3 + 2x^2\\).\n3. Subtract \\(x^3 + 2x^2\\) from \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) to get \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\).\n4. Divide the leading term of the new numerator \\(-4x^2\\) by the leading term of the denominator \\(x\\) to get \\(-4x\\).\n5. Multiply \\(-4x\\) by \\(x + 2\\) to get \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\).\n6. Subtract \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\) from \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\) to get \\(0\\).\n\nSo, the division gives us \\(x^2 - 4x\\) with no remainder, and the equation simplifies to:\n\\[5 = x^2 - 4x.\\]\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0.\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation, which we can factor as:\n\\[(x - 5)(x + 1) = 0.\\]\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\\[x = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1.\\]\nThe sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is:\n\\[5 + (-1) = 4.\\]\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{4}.\\]",
