      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2}\\), we start by simplifying the right-hand side. We do this by polynomial division of \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) by \\(x + 2\\).\n\nFirst, we divide the leading term of the numerator \\(x^3\\) by the leading term of the denominator \\(x\\) to get \\(x^2\\). Then we multiply \\(x^2\\) by \\(x + 2\\) to get \\(x^3 + 2x^2\\). We subtract this from the original polynomial:\n\n\\[\nx^3 - 2x^2 - 8x - (x^3 + 2x^2) = -4x^2 - 8x\n\\]\n\nNext, we divide the leading term of the new polynomial \\(-4x^2\\) by the leading term of the denominator \\(x\\) to get \\(-4x\\). Then we multiply \\(-4x\\) by \\(x + 2\\) to get \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\). We subtract this from \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\):\n\n\\[\n-4x^2 - 8x - (-4x^2 - 8x) = 0\n\\]\n\nSo, the quotient is \\(x^2 - 4x\\) and the remainder is 0. Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2} = x^2 - 4x\n\\]\n\nThe equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2}\\) simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n5 = x^2 - 4x\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x - 5 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe can factor this quadratic equation as:\n\n\\[\n(x - 5)(x + 1) = 0\n\\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\n\\[\nx - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nSo, the solutions are:\n\n\\[\nx = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is:\n\n\\[\n5 + (-1) = 4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
