      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2}\\), we first need to simplify the right-hand side of the equation. Notice that the numerator \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) can be factored. We can factor out an \\(x\\) from the numerator:\n\n\\[x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = x(x^2 - 2x - 8).\\]\n\nNext, we factor the quadratic expression \\(x^2 - 2x - 8\\):\n\n\\[x^2 - 2x - 8 = (x - 4)(x + 2).\\]\n\nSo, the numerator can be written as:\n\n\\[x(x - 4)(x + 2).\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[5 = \\frac{x(x - 4)(x + 2)}{x + 2}.\\]\n\nWe can cancel the \\(x + 2\\) terms in the numerator and the denominator, as long as \\(x \\neq -2\\):\n\n\\[5 = x(x - 4).\\]\n\nThis simplifies to the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0.\\]\n\nWe can factor this quadratic equation:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = (x - 5)(x + 1) = 0.\\]\n\nSetting each factor to zero gives us the solutions:\n\n\\[x - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x + 1 = 0,\\]\n\\[x = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1.\\]\n\nThe solutions to the equation are \\(x = 5\\) and \\(x = -1\\). The sum of these \\(x\\)-values is:\n\n\\[5 + (-1) = 4.\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
